<h2>K-Beauty in Seoul — The Source</h2>
<p>Korean beauty (K-beauty) has taken the world by storm — and Seoul is where it all starts. Products launch here months before they reach international markets. Prices are 30–50% cheaper than buying K-beauty abroad. And the sheer variety is staggering — you'll find thousands of products that never even make it to export. This is the pilgrimage every skincare enthusiast needs to make.</p>

<h2>🛍️ Where to Shop K-Beauty in Seoul</h2>

<h3>Myeongdong — The K-Beauty Capital</h3>
<p>The single best neighborhood for K-beauty shopping. The main street and side alleys are wall-to-wall with brand stores and street vendors selling beauty products. Every major Korean brand has a flagship here: Innisfree, Etude House, The Face Shop, Missha, Laneige, Sulwhasoo, and dozens more. Competition between stores means aggressive promotions — buy-one-get-one deals are common, and staff hand out free samples constantly.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Open daily until 10–11pm</li>
  <li>Free samples at every store — don't be shy about asking</li>
  <li>Tax refund available for purchases over ₩30,000 (bring your passport)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Olive Young — Korea's Beauty Superstore</h3>
<p>Think of Olive Young as a Korean Sephora meets drugstore. Multiple locations across Seoul (Myeongdong, Hongdae, Gangnam). You'll find both established brands and indie K-beauty brands that haven't gone international yet. The staff picks section is excellent — these are products Korean beauty editors actually recommend.</p>

<div class="tip-box">
  💡 <strong>Olive Young tip:</strong> Download the Olive Young app and get a membership card (free). You'll get points on every purchase and access to app-exclusive coupons. The Grand Sale happens twice a year — massive discounts sitewide.
</div>

<h3>Gangnam — Luxury & Derma Brands</h3>
<p>For high-end K-beauty and dermatologist brands, head to Gangnam. The Apgujeong neighborhood has luxury Korean beauty (Sulwhasoo, History of Whoo, Su:m37°). Gangnam is also where Korea's famous "beauty clinics" are concentrated — dermatology treatments, botox, and non-surgical procedures at a fraction of Western prices.</p>

<h3>Dongdaemun — Wholesale Beauty</h3>
<p>For quantity buyers, Dongdaemun's beauty wholesale district has cosmetics at trade prices. Great for buying gifts in bulk. Open 24 hours, busiest late at night.</p>

<h2>💄 What to Buy: The K-Beauty Essentials</h2>

<h3>Skincare</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Sunscreen:</strong> Korean sunscreens are genuinely superior. The texture is lighter, the finish is more elegant, and they're actually enjoyable to wear. Look for: Beauty of Joseon Relief Sun, Skin1004 Madagascar Centella, Round Lab Birch Juice.</li>
  <li><strong>Sheet Masks:</strong> Try them all. Mediheal, Leaders, SNP — boxes of 10 for ₩10,000–₩15,000.</li>
  <li><strong>Essence / Serum:</strong> SK-II is Japanese but the Korean equivalents (Missha Time Revolution, Cosrx Snail Mucin) are excellent at a fraction of the price.</li>
  <li><strong>Toner Pads:</strong> Pre-soaked cotton pads for exfoliation and hydration. Some by Mi, COSRX, and Neogen are particularly good.</li>
  <li><strong>Eye Cream:</strong> Korean eye creams are some of the best in the world and remarkably affordable.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Makeup</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Cushion Foundation:</strong> Korea invented this — a compact sponge soaked in liquid foundation. Perfect for on-the-go touch-ups. Try: IOPE Air Cushion, Laneige Neo Cushion.</li>
  <li><strong>Lip Products:</strong> Korea does lip tints better than anyone. Try: Romand Juicy Lasting Tint, Peripera Ink Velvet, 3CE Velvet Lip Color.</li>
  <li><strong>Eyebrow Products:</strong> Korean eyebrow trends are precise and natural. Etude House Draw My Brow is a cult classic.</li>
</ul>

<div class="tip-box">
  💡 <strong>Shopping strategy:</strong> Do a research walk first — look at prices without buying. Compare across Olive Young, Innisfree, and the individual brand stores. Then go back and buy. Stores often have different promotions and gift sets can change the value equation significantly.
</div>

<h2>💰 Budget Guide</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Sheet mask (single): ₩500–₩2,000</li>
  <li>Sheet mask (box of 10): ₩8,000–₩15,000</li>
  <li>Quality sunscreen: ₩12,000–₩22,000</li>
  <li>Mid-range serum: ₩20,000–₩45,000</li>
  <li>Premium cream (Sulwhasoo, History of Whoo): ₩80,000–₩300,000+</li>
  <li>Lip tint: ₩8,000–₩15,000</li>
</ul>

<h2>🧴 Brand Guide: Who's Who</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Budget (under ₩20,000):</strong> Etude House, Missha, The Saem, Skinfood, Nature Republic</li>
  <li><strong>Mid-range:</strong> Innisfree, Laneige, COSRX, Mediheal, Some By Mi</li>
  <li><strong>Premium:</strong> AHC, Dr.Jart+, Klairs, Purito, Beauty of Joseon</li>
  <li><strong>Luxury:</strong> Sulwhasoo, History of Whoo, Su:m37°, O HUI</li>
</ul>

<h2>✈️ Packing Your K-Beauty Haul</h2>
<p>K-beauty items are mostly liquids — plan your luggage accordingly:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Check baggage weight limits before you go on a K-beauty mission</li>
  <li>Bubble wrap or clothes to protect glass bottles</li>
  <li>Sheet masks are paper-thin — pack as many as you want in carry-on</li>
  <li>Liquid items over 100ml must go in checked luggage</li>
  <li>Tax refund on purchases over ₩30,000 — keep your receipts and claim at the airport</li>
</ul>

